The scoped emitter copied ONLY `.ts`. The preview window's document —
`out/vs/workbench/contrib/crusorai/browser/media/crusor-preview/index.html`,
the path FileAccess.asBrowserUri resolves for the frame — therefore existed
in src/ and NOWHERE in out/, which is the tree the app actually runs from:
the button opened a window pointing at a missing file, the site never
rendered, and every check stayed green. The sources hash covers media
SOURCES (it hashed that src file happily), the emit tests only grep emitted
JS, and out/ is gitignored so no diff ever showed the hole. The 541-file
crusorai-spa tree the Agent pane iframes had the same hole (0 files
emitted). Only the full `npm run compile` (gulpfile copyResources) ever
wrote media, so every machine that updated by pulling the committed bundle
got the JS and none of the media.

- emitter: copy every non-.ts file VERBATIM (raw bytes — fonts must not be
  text-round-tripped) and REFUSE TO SHIP when one does not land byte
  identical in out/
- audit: exit non-zero on an incomplete out/ (it printed 543 missing files
  line by line and still exited 0 — "reported but green" is what hid this)
- SOURCES_HASH + SOURCES_MANIFEST.json regenerated (the emitter's own bytes
  are hashed, so the recipe moved)
- runbook §5: the emitter carries the media, the audit is a verdict, and
  the emitter runs BEFORE the test gate
- tests: emitter media contract + audit verdict (2 new)

Proven by running the fixed emitter end-to-end on a clean worktree (stub
typescript, real control flow): 549 media copied, SPA 541 + preview-window
4 in out/, audit FAIL -> OK, and the engine's Python mirror of the hash
recipe agrees with the emitter's own stamp exactly (1f8a7f39…).

